use warnings;
use strict;

=head1 NAME

generic_entity - snmpd pass_persist for ENTITY-MIB, ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB, and ENTITY-STATE-MIB on unix

=head1 VERSION

0.1.0

=cut

our $VERSION = '0.1.0';

=head1 SYNOPSIS

generic_entity B<-u> [B<-d> <cache dir>] [B<--dmi>] [B<--smart>] [B<--smart-cache> <cache>]
[B<--smart-mode> <mode>] [B<--ipmi>][B<--state>] [B<--thresholds>]

generic_entity B<-p> <base OID> [B<-d> <cache dir>]

generic_entity B<-w> [B<-d> <cache dir>]

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Reports hardware inventory from the SMBIOS tables via dmidecode, disks
and their temperatures from the smart-v1 extend's cache or smartctl, and BMC sensors
via freeipmi, as ENTITY-MIB inventory, ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB sensors, and
ENTITY-STATE-MIB state.

Nothing in it is specific to a OS, so it is for the unix systems that
lack their own script, such as OpenBSD, NetBSD, and illumos. On Linux and
FreeBSD, linux_entity and freebsd_entity cover more. Only one of them can
be used on a host, as they answer for the same MIBs.

It works in two parts. Ran with B<-u> from cron as root, it gathers
everything and writes the cache. Ran with B<-p> by snmpd, it answers
requests from the cache, so snmpd never waits on dmidecode or the BMC.

=head1 SNMPD CONFIG

    # cron
    */5 * * * * root /etc/snmp/extends/generic_entity -u --dmi --smart --ipmi --state

    # snmpd.conf
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.47 /etc/snmp/extends/generic_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.47
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.99 /etc/snmp/extends/generic_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.99
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.131 /etc/snmp/extends/generic_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.131

One line is needed per MIB as they are in separate trees. B<-p> is told
the tree as snmpd does not pass along what it was registered for. The
.1.3.6.1.2.1.131 line is only needed with B<--state>. B<--thresholds>
needs one more, see L</THRESHOLDS>.

This needs net-snmp, as bsnmpd and OpenBSD's snmpd do not support
pass_persist.

Run B<-u> once before restarting snmpd so the cache exists. Without a
cache, nothing is returned.

=head1 REQUIREMENTS

dmidecode for B<--dmi>, on hardware with SMBIOS tables.

For B<--smart>, the smart-v1 extend ran from cron so its cache stays
current, with useSN set to 0 in its config, see B<--smart-mode>, or
smartmontools 7.0 or newer.

freeipmi for B<--ipmi>, plus a BMC the kernel can reach.

The tools are looked for in the path, then in /usr/local, /usr/pkg, and
/opt/local, as cron often has a minimal path.

=head1 FLAGS

=head2 -u

Update the cache. Needs root, as dmidecode and the BMC do. The chassis is
always present. The rest are added with the flags below.

=head2 --dmi

Add what the SMBIOS tables have, via dmidecode. The chassis gets its
vendor, product, serial, and BIOS version, and the motherboard, CPUs,
memory, and power supplies are added.

Power supplies come from the SMBIOS system power supply structures,
which many systems leave out. Their status is as of boot, as the BIOS
does not update it after, so no entStateTable row is added for them.

=head2 --smart

Add the disks, from the smart-v1 extend's cache or smartctl, see
B<--smart-mode>.

Each disk gets its serial, firmware, and model, plus a temperature
sensor named "$disk Temperature", with its temperature limit as a
threshold, see L</THRESHOLDS>, and with B<--state> a entStateTable row,
see L</STATE>.

=head2 --smart-cache <cache>

The smart-v1 cache to use. Defaults to /var/cache/smart, the plain JSON
one, not smart.snmp. Implies B<--smart>.

=head2 --smart-mode <mode>

Where the SMART info comes from. Implies B<--smart>.

- auto :: The smart-v1 cache if it exists, else smartctl. The default.

- extend :: The smart-v1 cache. It is as old as the last smart-v1 run.
  useSN should be set to 0 in the smart-v1 config. Disks are then keyed
  by the name from its config, such as ada0 or twl0,1, so a replaced disk
  keeps the same entity and sensor history. With useSN=1, they are keyed
  by serial, so a replaced disk shows up as a new entity, with the old
  one going away, and they are named after the device plus the serial.

- smartctl :: The disks come from C<smartctl --scan>, which includes those
  behind RAID controllers it knows how to reach, named after the device
  plus the type, such as "bus/0 megaraid,0". The rest are named after the
  device, such as sda. Disks that are asleep are skipped instead of being
  woken up. As nothing else lists the disks, they are missing from the
  cache until a run when they are awake.

=head2 --ipmi

Add the BMC, with its manufacturer, product ID, and firmware from
C<bmc-info>, and its sensors via C<ipmi-sensors>. Temperature, voltage,
current, power, and fan readings are reported. Discrete sensors and those
in other units, such as percent, are skipped.

=head2 --state

Add ENTITY-STATE-MIB state for the BMC with B<--ipmi> and the disks with
B<--smart>.

=head2 --thresholds

Add the CISCO-ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB entSensorThresholdTable, under
.1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.91, for sensors that have thresholds, which are the
B<--ipmi> and B<--smart> ones. See L</THRESHOLDS>.

=head2 -p <base OID>

Run as a snmpd pass_persist for the tree under the base OID.

=head2 -w

Print the cache the same as snmpwalk would, for debugging.

=head2 -d <cache dir>

The cache directory. Defaults to /var/cache/generic_entity. The cache is
entity.json in it, with state.json holding what is needed between runs.

=head1 ENTITIES

- 1 :: The chassis.

- 1000000000 and up :: Everything else. Each is given the next free index
  the first time it is seen, keyed by something stable such as the DIMM
  locator or the disk name. That is saved in state.json.
  Removing it renumbers everything.

=head1 THRESHOLDS

ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB has nowhere to put thresholds, so with B<--thresholds>
they go in the entSensorThresholdTable from CISCO-ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB. It is
indexed by the entPhysicalIndex of the sensor, then the threshold.

- 1 :: Lower non-recoverable, critical, less or equal.

- 2 :: Lower critical, major, less or equal.

- 3 :: Lower non-critical, minor, less or equal.

- 4 :: Upper non-critical, minor, greater or equal.

- 5 :: Upper critical, major, greater or equal.

- 6 :: Upper non-recoverable, critical, greater or equal.

Only the ones the BMC has are present. Disk temperatures only have 5, the
drive's temperature limit. The value uses the same scale and precision as
the sensor's entPhySensorValue. entSensorThresholdEvaluation is true if
the reading is past it. entSensorThresholdNotificationEnable is always
false, as no traps are sent.

It needs its own pass_persist line...

    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.91 /etc/snmp/extends/generic_entity -p .1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.91

=head1 STATE

With B<--state>, the entStateTable has a row for the BMC with B<--ipmi>
and each disk with B<--smart>.

- BMC :: entStateOper is enabled. entStateAlarm has critical set if any
  sensor is at or past a non-recoverable threshold, major for a critical
  one, and warning for a non-critical one.

- Disks :: entStateOper is disabled if smartctl could not read the disk
  and enabled if it could. entStateAlarm has critical set if the SMART
  health check did not pass, which includes smartctl not being able to
  read it, and major if it is over its temperature limit. A disk with no
  SMART health status, such as some virtual disks, never has critical set.

The rest of the columns are unknown.

=head1 INSTALL

Debian...

    apt-get install libjson-perl libfile-slurp-perl dmidecode freeipmi-tools smartmontools

FreeBSD...

    pkg install p5-JSON p5-File-Slurp dmidecode freeipmi smartmontools

Generic...

    cpanm JSON File::Slurp

=cut

